WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Two lab benches titrate the same hydrochloric acid

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.080
against the same standard.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.000
Each bench's titres agree within zero point zero

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.760
five millilitres.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.160
But bench A's average is twenty four point

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.040
three three, and bench B's is twenty four

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.600
point eight two.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.120
So how do you run a titration you

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.560
can trust, and spot what went wrong?

00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:25.606
The unknown is hydrochloric acid, roughly zero point

00:00:25.687 --> 00:00:27.286
one moles per litre.

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:30.246
The known is a sodium carbonate standard, made

00:00:30.327 --> 00:00:31.526
from the solid.

00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:33.286
A pipette puts an aliquot of it in

00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:35.259
the flask, and the acid goes in the

00:00:35.287 --> 00:00:36.406
burette.

00:00:36.487 --> 00:00:39.366
As in the titration video, carbonate takes two

00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:40.646
hydrochloric acid.

00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:44.586
Start with sodium carbonate dried in an oven

00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:47.026
and cooled in a desiccator, so none of

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:48.626
its mass is water.

00:00:48.987 --> 00:00:50.826
Weigh it into a beaker and record the

00:00:50.907 --> 00:00:54.106
exact mass: one point three six five six

00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:55.106
grams.

00:00:55.387 --> 00:00:57.626
Dissolve it, pour it through a funnel into

00:00:57.707 --> 00:01:00.986
a two hundred and fifty millilitre volumetric flask,

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:03.306
then rinse the beaker and funnel in, so

00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.466
every grain ends up inside.

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:08.986
Add water until the bottom of the meniscus

00:01:09.027 --> 00:01:11.466
sits on the mark, at eye level, then

00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:14.346
stopper and invert it many times, so every

00:01:14.427 --> 00:01:16.066
aliquot is the same.

00:01:16.507 --> 00:01:19.226
n equals m over M gives zero point

00:01:19.347 --> 00:01:22.266
zero one two eight eight four moles, and

00:01:22.427 --> 00:01:25.466
over zero point two five zero zero litres,

00:01:25.587 --> 00:01:28.186
that's zero point zero five one five four

00:01:28.427 --> 00:01:29.466
moles per litre.

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:31.986
Now the aliquot.

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:34.986
Rinse the pipette with a little carbonate solution,

00:01:35.107 --> 00:01:37.306
because water left inside would dilute it.

00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:39.786
Fill it with a filler to the mark,

00:01:39.867 --> 00:01:42.266
drain it into the conical flask, and touch

00:01:42.347 --> 00:01:44.346
the tip to the glass, but leave the

00:01:44.507 --> 00:01:47.386
last drop: the pipette is calibrated for that.

00:01:47.947 --> 00:01:50.186
The flask can be wet with distilled water,

00:01:50.307 --> 00:01:52.186
because water adds no carbonate.

00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:55.866
Add three drops of methyl orange: yellow.

00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:58.426
Next, the burette.

00:01:58.907 --> 00:02:01.626
Rinse it twice with a little hydrochloric acid,

00:02:01.707 --> 00:02:02.586
out through the tap.

00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:04.906
Fill it through a funnel, with its top

00:02:04.987 --> 00:02:07.786
below eye level, then take the funnel out,

00:02:07.947 --> 00:02:09.626
because drips could change the reading.

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:12.106
Open the tap to fill the tip, with

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:13.626
no air bubble left.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:16.433
Read it with your eye level with the

00:02:16.594 --> 00:02:18.033
bottom of the meniscus.

00:02:18.274 --> 00:02:20.433
On a burette the numbers get bigger as

00:02:20.514 --> 00:02:21.313
you go down.

00:02:21.794 --> 00:02:23.873
Look from above, and your line of sight

00:02:23.954 --> 00:02:26.273
meets the scale above the meniscus, where the

00:02:26.354 --> 00:02:28.833
numbers are smaller, so the reading comes out

00:02:28.954 --> 00:02:29.553
too low.

00:02:30.034 --> 00:02:31.073
That's parallax.

00:02:31.554 --> 00:02:33.313
The lines are a tenth of a millilitre

00:02:33.434 --> 00:02:36.673
apart, so estimate to the nearest half line,

00:02:36.754 --> 00:02:38.993
zero point zero five millilitres.

00:02:39.634 --> 00:02:42.433
This start reading is zero point four zero.

00:02:43.634 --> 00:02:46.353
The first run is a quick rough titration.

00:02:46.754 --> 00:02:48.673
It overshot to red at about twenty four

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:51.073
point nine, but now you know where the

00:02:51.274 --> 00:02:51.873
endpoint is.

00:02:52.354 --> 00:02:55.313
In each accurate run, add acid quickly to

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:58.833
about a millilitre short, then drop by drop,

00:02:58.914 --> 00:03:01.073
swirling the flask over the white tile.

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:04.113
Stop at the first permanent orange, and read

00:03:04.154 --> 00:03:05.153
the burette again.

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:09.406
Here are bench A's results: each titre is

00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:11.166
final minus initial.

00:03:11.647 --> 00:03:13.646
Run two came out at twenty four point

00:03:13.807 --> 00:03:16.286
six five, and the notebook says an air

00:03:16.367 --> 00:03:17.486
bubble left the tip.

00:03:18.047 --> 00:03:20.526
The bubble's volume was counted as acid, so

00:03:20.607 --> 00:03:22.126
that titre is too big.

00:03:22.607 --> 00:03:24.606
The cause is known, so that run is

00:03:24.687 --> 00:03:26.046
left out and repeated.

00:03:26.607 --> 00:03:28.766
The other three agree within zero point zero

00:03:28.927 --> 00:03:31.406
five, with a mean of twenty four point

00:03:31.487 --> 00:03:32.206
three three.

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:34.913
Now the calculation.

00:03:35.194 --> 00:03:38.393
The aliquot holds zero point zero five one

00:03:38.554 --> 00:03:41.913
five four times zero point zero two five

00:03:42.074 --> 00:03:45.193
zero zero litres: one point two eight eight

00:03:45.354 --> 00:03:47.753
five times ten to the minus three moles

00:03:47.834 --> 00:03:48.753
of carbonate.

00:03:48.954 --> 00:03:52.153
Times two: two point five seven seven times

00:03:52.234 --> 00:03:54.673
ten to the minus three moles of acid.

00:03:54.874 --> 00:03:57.673
Divide by the mean titre in litres: zero

00:03:57.914 --> 00:04:00.953
point one zero five nine moles per litre.

00:04:02.167 --> 00:04:04.006
So what happened on bench B?

00:04:04.487 --> 00:04:07.206
Its titres agreed, but each was about half

00:04:07.287 --> 00:04:08.726
a millilitre too big.

00:04:09.127 --> 00:04:11.606
Bench B rinsed its burette with distilled water

00:04:11.687 --> 00:04:12.806
before each fill.

00:04:13.207 --> 00:04:16.246
That water dilutes the acid, so more millilitres

00:04:16.287 --> 00:04:18.526
are needed to react with the same carbonate.

00:04:18.727 --> 00:04:21.446
A bigger titre means a smaller answer, because

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:23.606
the same moles of acid are divided by

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:27.446
more millilitres: zero point one zero three eight,

00:04:27.647 --> 00:04:29.006
about two per cent low.

00:04:29.207 --> 00:04:31.686
The fix: rinse with what's going in.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.860
In a titration, nothing is changed to test

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:36.580
its effect.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.860
You're measuring one thing, the titre, so keep

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:43.540
everything else the same: the aliquot, the standard,

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:45.780
the indicator and how much of it, and

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:46.980
the endpoint colour.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.160
Validity: does the method answer the question?

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.920
Yes, if everything but the titre is controlled,

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.000
and the one to two ratio holds at

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:58.200
this endpoint.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:03.080
Reliability: concordant titres, with a faulty run repeated.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.680
Accuracy: bench B shows that titres can agree

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:07.960
and still be wrong.

00:05:09.167 --> 00:05:10.286
The risk assessment.

00:05:10.847 --> 00:05:14.686
Solid sodium carbonate causes serious eye irritation, so

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:17.406
wear safety glasses and avoid its dust.

00:05:17.647 --> 00:05:20.926
The dilute solutions are below their classification limits,

00:05:21.007 --> 00:05:22.846
but glasses stay on for splashes.

00:05:23.327 --> 00:05:26.126
Methyl orange powder is toxic if swallowed, but

00:05:26.207 --> 00:05:28.366
a few drops of dilute indicator are low

00:05:28.447 --> 00:05:29.086
risk.

00:05:29.327 --> 00:05:31.566
Fill the burette below eye level, and use

00:05:31.647 --> 00:05:32.686
a pipette filler.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:34.540
Your turn.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.220
A student rinses her pipette with distilled water,

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.020
and then, without drying it, uses it to

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.660
transfer the sodium carbonate aliquot.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.820
Everything else is done correctly.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.780
Explain the effect on her calculated concentration of

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.380
hydrochloric acid.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:51.820
Pause here to answer.

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:05.273
The water left inside dilutes the aliquot, so

00:06:05.354 --> 00:06:07.313
it holds fewer moles of carbonate than the

00:06:07.354 --> 00:06:08.713
calculation assumes.

00:06:09.274 --> 00:06:11.673
That needs less acid, so the titre is

00:06:11.754 --> 00:06:12.313
smaller.

00:06:12.874 --> 00:06:15.513
The calculation divides the full moles by that

00:06:15.594 --> 00:06:18.393
smaller titre, so the answer is too high.

00:06:19.034 --> 00:06:22.313
Saying "diluted, so it's too low" forgets that

00:06:22.354 --> 00:06:24.073
the acid is the unknown.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:25.860
One more.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:29.140
Before adding the aliquot, a student rinses the

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:32.180
conical flask with the sodium carbonate solution.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.100
Everything else is done correctly.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:37.540
Explain the effect on her calculated concentration of

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:38.740
hydrochloric acid.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:40.740
Pause here to answer.

00:06:51.167 --> 00:06:53.566
The rinse leaves a film of carbonate solution

00:06:53.607 --> 00:06:56.046
in the flask, so it holds more moles

00:06:56.127 --> 00:06:57.606
than the aliquot alone.

00:06:57.887 --> 00:07:00.446
That needs more acid, so the titre is

00:07:00.527 --> 00:07:01.166
bigger.

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:04.366
The calculation divides the aliquot's moles by that

00:07:04.447 --> 00:07:07.806
bigger titre, so the answer is too low.

00:07:07.887 --> 00:07:09.646
Rinsing with the solution is right for the

00:07:09.767 --> 00:07:11.646
pipette, not the flask.

00:07:12.867 --> 00:07:15.986
So, for a titration you can trust: rinse

00:07:16.067 --> 00:07:18.866
with what's going in, clear the bubble, read

00:07:18.947 --> 00:07:22.306
at eye level, and average concordant titres.

00:07:22.507 --> 00:07:24.866
Trace any error through the titre.

00:07:25.107 --> 00:07:27.986
Next: titration calculation practice.

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:30.640
Here are the key notes.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.800
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:33.680
to copy them.
